READING — algorithm / pseudocode
================================

Companion to `reading_desc`, which is normative: every procedure here implements
a numbered section of it and cites that section. Where the two disagree,
`reading_desc` wins and this file is wrong.

Language-agnostic, but named so it can be implemented directly against the
`optimal_lambda/` package. Points marked [OPEN — On] correspond to the open
points of `reading_desc` §10 and must not be silently resolved in code.

CITATION CONVENTION. A bare §n refers to a section OF THIS FILE. A section of the
prose spec is written `reading_desc §n`, except in a section HEADING, where the
parenthesised reference gives the prose section that heading implements — so
"3.1 Contraction (§4.1)" means this file's §3.1 implements reading_desc §4.1.

Two things this file exists to make concrete, because they are the parts the
prose leaves distributed:

  - the DRIVER: what one reading step actually consists of, end to end, with the
    ontology set maintained in step (§7 below);
  - the SILENCE / APPROVAL boundary: which graph reductions the machine performs
    on its own and which require the user's act (§6.3).


0. Data structures
------------------

    Entity      := IRI of a knowledge-graph node        # a "type" A, B, C...
    Relation    := (subject: Entity, object: Entity)    # an arrow A -> B

    Term        := Var(iri, label)
                 | App(func: Term, arg: Term)
                 | Abs(var: Var, body: Term)     # ONTOLOGY LAYER ONLY (I6)

    Occurrence  := a use of a subgraph, identified by the path taken to reach it.
                   NOT interchangeable with the subgraph: reflection casts a type
                   onto an occurrence (reading_desc §4.2), so two occurrences of
                   one shared subgraph may carry different types.

    Pointer     := handle designating an EDGE of a graph, together with the
                   OCCURRENCE it is reached through. Must survive graph
                   rewriting, so not a raw end id: mint a stable id per site.

    OpenReading := record {
                     graph      : Graph        # G(t), a GAL bus sharing graph
                     term       : Term         # t, kept in step with graph
                     pointers   : set[Pointer] # Pr — never shrinks (reading_desc §1)
                     ontologies : set[Ontology]
                   }

    ClosedReading := record {                  # reading_desc §1
                     graph      : Graph
                     term       : Term
                     ontologies : set[Ontology]   # STORED on save (O7 resolved)
                     name       : str
                   }                              # NO pointer set

    Ontology    := record {                    # reading_desc §8.1
                     graph    : Graph          # CONTAINS abstractions
                     term     : Term
                     pointers : set[Pointer]   # corresponds ELEMENTWISE to the
                   }                           # reading's own pointer set

    Store       := closed readings, addressable by name.
    AStore      := saved abstractions, queryable BY BOUND-VARIABLE TYPE.

A reading is a PROCESS: OPEN while built, CLOSED once saved. A closed reading is
de facto a reading with one pointer at its graph root, so closing collapses the
pointer set and re-opening re-mints it.

    PERSISTENCE NOTE. `kg_store.save_readings_db` holds (name, term) pairs only.
    That suffices for graph and term but NOT for the ontology set, which §7.1 and
    §3.1 both read. Extending the schema is a prerequisite, not optional.


1. Typing  —  [t] at an occurrence
-----------------------------------

`term_utils._term_type` implements the three rules of reading_desc §2 and is
normative for UNREFLECTED material:

    function TYPE_OF(t: Term) -> Entity:
        while t is App:
            if t.func is Abs:
                return t.func.var.iri          # [(lam a) t] = [a]
            t = t.arg                          # [app(a,b)]  = [b]
        if t is Var:
            return t.iri                       # [a]
        return UNDEFINED

    [OPEN — O1] TYPE_OF IS INSUFFICIENT ON ITS OWN. Reflection casts A and B onto
    the two occurrences of one shared t, so [t] is not a function of the term
    (reading_desc §4.2). The type must be read AT THE OCCURRENCE:

    function TYPE_AT(R, p: Pointer) -> Entity:
        if p carries a CAST from a reflection:      # set by REFLECT, §3.2
            return that cast type
        return TYPE_OF( SUBTERM_AT(R, p) )

    Restating reading_desc §2's rules to take an occurrence argument is
    mechanical and has not been done; until it is, carry the cast on the pointer
    as above.


2. Initial step  (reading_desc §3)
-----------------------------------

Two forms. Both yield exactly ONE pointer, so the steps of §3 apply from the
outset.

    function INIT_FROM_TYPE(A: Entity) -> OpenReading:
        a = fresh Var of type A                       # [a] == A
        G = compile_term(a)                           # optimal_lambda.compile
        p = POINTER_TO_ROOT_EDGE(G)
        R = OpenReading(graph=G, term=a, pointers={p}, ontologies={})
        R.ontologies = INIT_ONTS_FROM_TYPE(a, p)      # §7.1
        return R

    function INIT_FROM_CLOSED(cr: ClosedReading) -> OpenReading:
        (G, t) = DEEP_COPY(cr.graph, cr.term)    # copy: cr stays immutable
        p = POINTER_TO_ROOT_EDGE(G)
        R = OpenReading(graph=G, term=t, pointers={p}, ontologies={})
        R.ontologies = INIT_ONTS_FROM_CLOSED(cr, p)   # §7.1
        return R

INIT_FROM_TYPE is the only point at which knowledge of the graph enters from
outside the calculus (search/browse). INIT_FROM_CLOSED needs no justification —
it is the user's own prior work.


3. The steps  (reading_desc §4)
--------------------------------

Exactly two: contraction (two options) and reflection. Every step acts at ONE
pointer, actPtr, and leaves the others untouched.

3.1 Contraction  —  one case, two options  (§4.1)
--------------------------------------------------

THE OPTIONS ADMIT DIFFERENT OPERANDS. Option 1 takes an entity, a closed reading,
or an abstraction title; option 2 takes an ENTITY ONLY. Intended, not an omission:
a closed reading may be what the user answers WITH, never what they ask FROM.

    SHARE_GRAFTED_READING := false            # [OPEN — O6] default: copy

    function OPERAND(sel) -> (Graph, Term, Entity):
        if sel is an Entity B:
            v = fresh Var of type B
            return (compile_term(v), v, B)
        if sel is a ClosedReading cr:                      # option 1 only
            if SHARE_GRAFTED_READING:
                return (SHARED_REF(cr.graph), cr.term, TYPE_OF(cr.term))
            (g, t) = DEEP_COPY(cr.graph, cr.term)
            return (g, t, TYPE_OF(t))
        if sel is an AbstractionTitle title:               # option 1 only
            # The READING sees only the title and treats it as an entity
            # (reading_desc §8.2). The positive form enters on the ONTOLOGY
            # side, via enrichment — see §7.2.
            v = fresh Var of type TYPE_OF_TITLE(title)
            return (compile_term(v), v, TYPE_OF_TITLE(title))

    function LEGAL_CONTRACT(R, actPtr, sel, option) -> bool:
        A = TYPE_AT(R, actPtr)
        B = TYPE_OF_OPERAND(sel)
        if option == 1:
            # the user stays at the arrow's SUBJECT: A asks, B answers
            return sel is not an Entity or EXISTS_RELATION(A, B)
        else:
            require sel is an Entity                       # option 2: §4.1
            # the user stays at the arrow's OBJECT: B asks, A answers
            return EXISTS_RELATION(B, A)

    # [OPEN — O5] For a closed-reading or title operand there is no entity
    # endpoint, so only the type precondition is checked. Option 2 is unaffected,
    # being entity-only.

    procedure CONTRACT(R, actPtr, sel, option):
        require LEGAL_CONTRACT(R, actPtr, sel, option)
        t1 = SUBTERM_AT(R, actPtr)                # TYPE_AT(R, actPtr) == A
        (gSel, tSel, B) = OPERAND(sel)

        if option == 1:                           # A asks, B answers
            t3  = App(t1, tSel)                   # operand in ARGUMENT position
            new = App_node(G(t1), gSel)
        else:                                     # B asks, A answers
            t3  = App(tSel, t1)                   # operand in FUNCTION position
            new = App_node(gSel, G(t1))

        REPLACE_SUBGRAPH(R.graph, at=actPtr, old=G(t1), new=new)
        R.term = REPLACE_SUBTERM(R.term, t1, t3)
        REBIND(actPtr, to=edge_of(t3))            # other pointers untouched (I2)

        ONTS_AFTER_CONTRACT(R, actPtr, sel, option, A, B)     # §7.3
        return R

    Post: option 1 -> TYPE_AT(R, actPtr) == B    (the type moved to B)
          option 2 -> TYPE_AT(R, actPtr) == A    (UNCHANGED — the user occupies
                                                  both places; the pointer names A)
    |Pr| unchanged by either option.

3.2 Reflection  (§4.2)
-----------------------

    function LEGAL_REFLECTION(R, actPtr, A, B) -> bool:
        C = TYPE_AT(R, actPtr)
        # [OPEN — O2] Available as a PRECONDITION only: nothing in the result
        # holds the cast to it, so reflection may retype a shared subterm freely.
        return EXISTS_RELATION(C, A) and EXISTS_RELATION(C, B)

    procedure REFLECT(R, actPtr, A: Entity, B: Entity):
        require LEGAL_REFLECTION(R, actPtr, A, B)
        t = SUBTERM_AT(R, actPtr)                 # TYPE_AT(R, actPtr) == C

        # NO fresh variables are emitted. Both branches ARE the shared t.
        fan = NEW_SHARING_FANIN(over=G(t))        # cf. _Compiler._fan_in:
                                                  #   arity-3 INTERNAL fan,
                                                  #   marked on the OFFSET wire
        t2 = App(t, t)                            # t occurs twice, exists once
        REPLACE_SUBGRAPH(R.graph, at=actPtr, old=G(t), new=G(t2))
        R.term = REPLACE_SUBTERM(R.term, t, t2)

        # The pointers designate the AUX-PORT EDGES — the two OCCURRENCES of t —
        # NOT the enclosing application node. §4 of this file depends on it.
        pA = POINTER_TO_EDGE_AT(fan, port="grey")     # LEFT-UP  aux port
        pB = POINTER_TO_EDGE_AT(fan, port="black")    # RIGHT-UP aux port
        SET_CAST(pA, A)                               # §1 above: cast rides on
        SET_CAST(pB, B)                               #     the pointer

        R.pointers = (R.pointers - {actPtr}) union {pA, pB}

        ONTS_AFTER_REFLECT(R, actPtr, A, B)           # §7.4
        return R

    Post: exactly one G(t) in the graph, reached through the fan-in (I5);
          |Pr| increased by 1; TYPE_AT(R,pA) == A, TYPE_AT(R,pB) == B.

    Why OFFSET: the sharing fan-in must sit on OFFSET while the syntactic λ/@
    fans sit on COMMAND (the rightmost wire). Same wire => R1 ANNIHILATE (wrong,
    the shared subject is consumed once); different wires => R4 DUPLICATE (right).

3.3 Saving  (closing a reading)
--------------------------------

    procedure SAVE(R: OpenReading, name: str) -> ClosedReading:
        # Total: closable from any state, whatever the pointer set
        #   (reading_desc §1).
        # Closing COLLAPSES the pointer set; ontologies ARE kept (O7).
        return ClosedReading(graph=R.graph, term=R.term,
                             ontologies=R.ontologies, name=name)

    A closed reading is then available in two ways, and only these: re-opened by
    INIT_FROM_CLOSED (§2), or used as an OPTION 1 contraction operand (§3.1).


4. Building argument-position sharing  (reading_desc §7.2)
-----------------------------------------------------------

Worth stating as a procedure because it is the one construction whose
correctness depends on where reflection's pointers land.

    procedure BUILD_ARG_SHARING(C, A, B) -> OpenReading:
        R = INIT_FROM_TYPE(C)                       # term: t
        p = the single pointer of R.pointers

        REFLECT(R, p, A, B)                         # term: app(t,t), t shared
        (pA, pB) = the two new pointers             # the two OCCURRENCES of t

        CONTRACT(R, pA, sel=Entity(A), option=2)    # t |-> app(a, t)
        CONTRACT(R, pB, sel=Entity(B), option=2)    # t |-> app(b, t)
        # term: app(app(a,t), app(b,t)) — t SHARED, in ARGUMENT position of both
        return R

Option 2 grafts AROUND the shared occurrence, which is what flips t from function
into argument position. Option 1 could not: it grafts INTO argument position and
would push t further into function position. Since reflection emits no
scaffolding, nothing needs discharging afterwards.


5. The ontology layer — shape  (reading_desc §8.1–8.2)
-------------------------------------------------------

An ontology is a MODEL IN WHICH THE READING IS VALID: a graph whose reduction
REPLAYS the reading, contraction <-> R1, reflection <-> R4. Its pointer set
corresponds ELEMENTWISE to the reading's.

    function CORRESPONDING_POINTER(o: Ontology, p: Pointer) -> Pointer:
        # Implement explicitly — carry a reading-pointer id on each ontology
        # pointer — rather than re-deriving positionally. Every update depends
        # on this correspondence totally (§8.1).

5.1 Which side of an abstraction
---------------------------------

    TITLE          the negative form. What a READING sees and contracts, exactly
                   like an entity (this file §3.1, OPERAND).
    POSITIVE FORM  G(lam a.t). What ONTOLOGIES are built from; invisible to a
                   reading.

    function TYPE_OF_ABSTRACTION(q) -> Entity:
        return q.var.iri              # [(lam a) t] = [a] — the BOUND VARIABLE

    function ABSTRACTIONS_OF_TYPE(A: Entity) -> set:
        # A finite concrete lookup; cf. widgets._record_contains_iri, which
        # tests `term.var.iri == iri`. NOTE the retyping: an abstraction's type
        # is its bound variable's, which DIFFERS from its body's.
        return { q in AStore : TYPE_OF_ABSTRACTION(q) == A }


6. Reduction discipline  (reading_desc §8.5)
---------------------------------------------

6.1 Pointers under enrichment
------------------------------

If p designates a and a is replaced by the positive form lamA with
[a] == [lamA], the new pointer set contains a pointer to lamA. THE POINTER
FOLLOWS THE MATERIAL.

6.2 Pointered configurations
-----------------------------

A pointer of P designates an abstraction and immediately above that edge is:

    a fan-in on the SAME (rightmost) wire        -> R1 applicable
    a fan-in on a DIFFERENT (more-left) wire     -> R4 applicable
        i.e. the SHARING node

Both are NON-SILENT by §6.3, so both require the user's act — which is exactly
what §7.3 and §7.4 supply. A pointer may also sit on a NON-abstraction, in which
case nothing reduces and no stability question arises.

6.3 WHICH REDUCTIONS ARE SILENT — the approval boundary
--------------------------------------------------------

A STRUCTURAL test on the marked wire. Not a test on where pointers are.

    function IS_SYNTACTIC(fan) -> bool:
        # A fan is a syntactic λ/@ node exactly when it is marked on its
        # RIGHTMOST wire. Verified: compile.py builds λ and @ as
        # new_fan(2, 1, ...) — width 2, main 1, so main == width-1 — while
        # _fan_in builds sharing as new_fan(INITIAL_ROOT_WIDTH, OFFSET, ...) —
        # width 3, main 1, whose rightmost slot is 2 (COMMAND).
        return fan.main == fan.width("principal") - 1

    function IS_SILENT(redex) -> bool:
        if redex.rule in {2, 3, 5, 6}:  return true      # ALWAYS silent
        if redex.rule == 1:             return not IS_SYNTACTIC_WIRE(redex)
        if redex.rule == 4:             return not IS_SYNTACTIC(redex.fan_out)

    where for rule 1 the test is on the wire the two fans meet on: on the
    RIGHTMOST wire they are the syntactic λ and @, so the reduction is a real
    BETA-STEP and is NOT silent; elsewhere it is.
    For rule 4, a rightmost fan-out is an ABSTRACTION node and the duplication
    IS the reflection, so NOT silent; a non-rightmost fan-out is a SHARING node
    and it is silent.

    procedure REDUCE_SILENTLY(g: Graph):
        # Perform every silent reduction available, to a fixpoint. The machine
        # does this on its own initiative.
        loop until no change:
            for each redex in find_redexes(g):
                if IS_SILENT(redex):  apply(redex)

    EVERY NON-SILENT REDUCTION MUST BE APPROVED BY THE USER DURING READING. That
    is the whole coupling between the layers: rightmost-wire R1 IS a contraction
    the user performed, rightmost-fan-out R4 IS a reflection they performed. The
    ontology layer never fires either on its own initiative — which is what makes
    an ontology a model OF THAT READING rather than an independent computation.

    CONSEQUENCE: REDUCE_SILENTLY can never run the ontology ahead of the reading,
    since it cannot perform a beta-step or a reflection by construction.


7. Maintaining the ontology set
--------------------------------

Per reading step: UPDATE (§7.3/§7.4), then ENRICH (§7.2).

7.1 Initialisation  (§8.3)
---------------------------

    function INIT_ONTS_FROM_TYPE(a: Var, p: Pointer) -> set[Ontology]:
        o = Ontology(graph=compile_term(a), term=a,
                     pointers={ptr to the edge G(a), corresponding to p})
        onts = {o}
        ENRICH_ALL(onts)
        return onts

    function INIT_ONTS_FROM_CLOSED(cr: ClosedReading, p: Pointer)
            -> set[Ontology]:
        # O7: the ontologies are STORED, not re-derived. Re-deriving was
        # rejected — stored ontologies are SURVIVORS of refutation over cr's
        # whole construction, which enrichment cannot recover.
        onts = DEEP_COPY(cr.ontologies)
        for each o in onts:
            COLLAPSE_POINTERS_TO_ROOT(o, corresponding_to=p)   # mirror §2
        ENRICH_ALL(onts)
        return onts

7.2 Enrichment  (§8.4)
-----------------------

After EVERY step: wherever a pointed subterm is a VARIABLE sitting in an
application, the POSITIVE FORM of an abstraction of matching type may be
substituted for it, yielding a further candidate.

    procedure ENRICH_ALL(onts: set[Ontology]):
        for each o in SNAPSHOT(onts):          # SNAPSHOT is load-bearing
            ENRICH(o, o.pointers, onts)

    procedure ENRICH(o, P, onts):
        p = HEAD(P)
        if p == null: return
        let p designate the subterm tp of o.term

        ENRICH(o, P - p, onts)                 # the remaining pointers

        # (a) tp in FUNCTION position of an application
        if o.term has a subterm App(tp, _) and tp is a Var:
            for each q in CANDIDATES(tp):
                o2 = o with G(tp) replaced by G(q)
                pq = pointer to q in o2
                o2.pointers = (P - p) union {pq}     # the question is a NEW
                onts.add(o2)                         #   POSITION
                ENRICH(o2, P - p, onts)

        # (b) tp in ARGUMENT position, function side t1 a Var
        if o.term has a subterm App(t1, tp) and t1 is a Var:
            for each q in CANDIDATES(t1):
                o2 = o with G(t1) replaced by G(q)
                o2.pointers = P                      # pointer set UNCHANGED
                onts.add(o2)
                ENRICH(o2, P - p, onts)

    function CANDIDATES(v: Var) -> set:
        # A NAMED title collapses the quantifier to that one abstraction; only
        # an unnamed position branches over all of matching type (§8.4).
        if v arose from an ABSTRACTION TITLE the user named:
            return { POSITIVE_FORM_OF(that title) }
        return ABSTRACTIONS_OF_TYPE( TYPE_OF(v) )

    SNAPSHOT MATTERS: ENRICH_ALL iterates while ENRICH adds. Without a snapshot
    taken before the pass, new ontologies are re-enriched in the same pass and,
    since each substitution can create fresh matching material, the pass need not
    terminate.

    TERMINATION: given the snapshot, recursion is strictly on the SHRINKING
    pointer set — every call passes P - p, and a new ontology is enriched only
    for the REMAINING pointers. No abstraction is substituted inside one
    introduced in the same pass. Depth bounded by |P|.

    THE (a)/(b) ASYMMETRY follows [app(a,b)] = [b]: in function position the
    substituted abstraction becomes a new POSITION; in argument position P is
    left alone.

    THE ABSTRACTION TITLE OPERAND IS COVERED HERE, and needs no case in §7.3:
    the title's counterpart in the explicit layer is its positive form, and
    substituting it at the pointer is what enrichment does.

7.3 Update on a contraction  (§8.6)
------------------------------------

Two branches. An ontology in which R1 does not fire is KEPT — the distinction is
between CONTRADICTED and merely SILENT.

    procedure ONTS_AFTER_CONTRACT(R, actPtr, sel, option, A, B):
        newOnts = {}
        for each o(G(t), P) in R.ontologies:
            p = CORRESPONDING_POINTER(o, actPtr)
            if p == null: continue

            if option == 1:
                # p must designate ta inside App(ta, tb), [ta]==A, [tb]==B
                if not (o.term has App(ta,tb) with p designating ta
                        and TYPE_OF(ta)==A and TYPE_OF(tb)==B):
                    continue                       # REFUTED (§7.5)

                if not R1_APPLICABLE(o.graph, p):          # ta not an abstraction
                    o2 = o with pointers = (P - {p}) union {ptr to App(ta,tb)}
                    newOnts.add(o2)                # SILENT, not contradicted
                else:
                    if sel is an Entity or an AbstractionTitle:
                        pb = pointer to tb
                        APPLY_R1(o.graph, p); REDUCE_SILENTLY(o.graph)
                        o2 = o with pointers = (P - {p}) union {REBASED(pb)}
                        newOnts.add(o2)

                    if sel is a ClosedReading cr:
                        # Cartesian product with cr's own ontologies.
                        # [OPEN — RN2] state what happens when neither sub-case
                        # below applies: currently the ontology is dropped, which
                        # contradicts the non-firing branch above.
                        if tb is a Var b:
                            for each ocr in cr.ontologies:
                                g1 = o.graph with b replaced by G(ocr.term)
                                APPLY_R1(g1, at the redex App(G(ta), G(ocr.term)))
                                REDUCE_SILENTLY(g1)
                                o2 = Ontology(graph=g1, ...,
                                     pointers=(P-{p}) union
                                              {REBASED(ptr to ocr.term)})
                                newOnts.add(o2)     # [OPEN — RN5] REBASED: the
                                                    # material may have been
                                                    # rewritten by the reduction
                        else:
                            # [OPEN — RN3] The intended test is that tb ALREADY
                            # IS one of cr's ontology-terms (arrived by an
                            # earlier graft or enrichment). It cannot literally
                            # BE an ontology of cr — different graphs.
                            if tb IS_ALREADY one of cr's ontology-terms:
                                APPLY_R1(o.graph, at App(G(ta), G(tb)))
                                REDUCE_SILENTLY(o.graph)
                                o2 = o with pointers = (P - {p}) union
                                                       {REBASED(ptr to tb)}
                                newOnts.add(o2)

            else:   # option 2 — entity operand only
                # p designates tb, the ARGUMENT; the R1 REDEX is at ta.
                if not (o.term has App(ta,tb) with p designating tb
                        and TYPE_OF(ta)==A and TYPE_OF(tb)==B):
                    continue                       # REFUTED

                if not R1_APPLICABLE_AT(o.graph, ta):      # test at ta, NOT at p
                    newOnts.add(o)                 # SILENT; P unchanged
                else:
                    APPLY_R1(o.graph, at=ta); REDUCE_SILENTLY(o.graph)
                    o2 = o with pointers = REBASE_ALL(P, onto=o.graph)
                    newOnts.add(o2)                # P unchanged: the reader
                                                   # does not move (§4.1)

        R.ontologies = newOnts                     # REFUTATION by omission
        ENRICH_ALL(R.ontologies)
        CHECK_EMPTY(R)                             # §7.5

    OPTION 2, TWO POINTS. The applicability test is at ta, NOT at the pointer —
    option 1 is the case where the two coincide. And P is carried over UNCHANGED
    because the reader does not move, but the pointers must be REBASED onto the
    rewritten graph: "unchanged" means the same positions, not the same edge ids.

7.4 Update on a reflection  (§8.7)
-----------------------------------

Also two branches — but here BOTH demand real structure: the required sharing
must already be present either way.

    procedure ONTS_AFTER_REFLECT(R, actPtr, A, B):
        newOnts = {}
        for each o(G(t), P) in R.ontologies:
            p = CORRESPONDING_POINTER(o, actPtr)
            if p == null: continue
            let p designate toc

            if not (o.term has App(ta,tb) with TYPE_OF(ta)==A, TYPE_OF(tb)==B,
                    TYPE_OF(toc)==C
                    and IS_SHARED_IN_BOTH(o.graph, toc, ta, tb)):
                continue                            # REFUTED

            if toc is an Abs and R4_APPLICABLE(o.graph, p):
                APPLY_R4(o.graph, p); REDUCE_SILENTLY(o.graph)
                # The new pointers designate NODES among the four fans R4 made:
                # the two COPIES OF THE ABSTRACTION's fan (see below).
                o2 = o with pointers = (P - {p}) union
                        { ptr to LEFT abstraction fan node,
                          ptr to RIGHT abstraction fan node }
                newOnts.add(o2)

            else if HAS_SHARING_FANIN_ABOVE(o.graph, toc):
                # NOTHING REDUCES. G(t) unchanged; the new pointers designate the
                # EDGES at the existing fan-in's two top ports.
                o2 = o with pointers = (P - {p}) union
                        { ptr to TOP LEFT  fan-in edge,
                          ptr to TOP RIGHT fan-in edge }
                newOnts.add(o2)

        R.ontologies = newOnts
        ENRICH_ALL(R.ontologies)
        CHECK_EMPTY(R)

    IS_SHARED_IN_BOTH IS A SEPARATE, STRICTER TEST than R4's own applicability.
    `rules.py:rule4_fan_fan_diff` fires whenever two fans meet
    principal-to-principal on DIFFERENT main wires, and its EFFECT is to
    replicate each onto the other's grey/black branches — the reducer CREATES
    duplication rather than requiring it. So implement this filter separately;
    do NOT read it off find_redexes.

    WHICH FANS THE TWO POINTERS DESIGNATE after R4: the rule REMOVES both fans
    that met and creates FOUR. Two are copies of the ABSTRACTION's fan,
    replicated onto the sharing fan's grey/black branches, carrying the
    abstraction's `role` and `iri`; their principals reach what were the sharing
    fan's externals. THOSE TWO are the targets. The other two are copies of the
    SHARING fan and are not pointer targets.

    NO CASCADE RISK: R4 removes only the two fans that met and re-attaches all
    four captured branch externals (`_capture_ports` / `_finalize`). A pointer
    elsewhere in P is undisturbed, so every step touches exactly the one pointer
    it acts at.

7.5 Refutation and the empty set  (§8.8)
-----------------------------------------

An ontology is discarded BY NEVER BEING ADDED to newOnts. There is no discard
statement; refutation's strength is exactly the strictness of the conditions.

THE OUTER STRUCTURAL TEST IS THE STRONG GATE. It demands the ontology ALREADY
CONTAIN the material the reading is about to build, and since enrichment
substitutes abstractions but NEVER ADDS APPLICATIONS, an ontology passes only if
some abstraction BODY supplies the needed argument position.

    Worked case: reading (a b), contracting B -> C, abstractions (lam a).(a d),
    (lam b).(b e), (lam c).(c f). The candidate ((lam b).(b e)) d models (a b),
    but at the B -> C step the pointer designates the WHOLE term, which has no
    enclosing application — outer test fails, refuted. No body supplies a c, so
    all candidates die and the set goes EMPTY.

    procedure CHECK_EMPTY(R):
        if R.ontologies is empty:
            SIGNAL("this reading has no valid model")   # distinguished state,
                                                        # NOT an assertion failure

AN EMPTY SET IS MEANINGFUL: no available explicit material can express what this
user is doing — the reading has outrun its abstractions. Surface it.

    [OPEN — O11] Whether to record CONFIRMATION per ontology (a count of actual
    firings) so that "survivors are better approximations" tracks confirmation
    rather than mere survival. A ranking refinement.
    [OPEN — O10] Whether a size bound is needed. Enrichment branches per pointer
    per step, and option 1's closed-reading case multiplies by |cr.ontologies|;
    against that, the outer test refutes heavily. Unsettled.


8. Driver
---------

    procedure RUN_READING():
        # initial step (§2)
        if user starts from a type:
            R = INIT_FROM_TYPE(user picks an Entity)
        else:
            R = INIT_FROM_CLOSED(user picks a ClosedReading from Store)

        loop until the user stops:
            actPtr = user picks a Pointer from R.pointers   # where they STAY
            A      = TYPE_AT(R, actPtr)                     # at the OCCURRENCE

            op = user picks: contraction | reflection

            if op == contraction:
                # Offer, built ONLY from what the user already knows:
                #   outgoing arrows A -> X   (option 1, entity operand)
                #   incoming arrows X -> A   (option 2, entity operand)
                #   closed readings from Store        (option 1 only)
                #   abstraction titles of a type      (option 1 only)
                (sel, option) = user picks an operand and an option
                CONTRACT(R, actPtr, sel, option)
            else:
                (A2, B2) = user picks two relations out of A
                REFLECT(R, actPtr, A2, B2)

            ASSERT_INVARIANTS(R)

        if the user saves:
            SAVE(R, name)          # closes it; ontologies persist (§3.3)

    NOTE WHAT THE DRIVER DOES NOT DO. It never reduces R.graph — the READING
    LAYER NEVER REDUCES (§9). All reduction happens inside the ontology updates,
    on ontology graphs, and only the silent part happens without the user.


9. Invariant check
------------------

    procedure ASSERT_INVARIANTS(R):
        for p in R.pointers:
            assert DESIGNATES_LIVE_EDGE(R.graph, p)          # I1
            assert TYPE_AT(R, p) is defined                  # I3
        assert R.graph.check_integrity() == []               # optimal_lambda
        assert R.term contains no Abs                        # I6
        assert exactly one copy of each shared subject       # I5
        # Pr is never empty (§1), but that follows from no operation removing a
        # pointer rather than needing a check.

        # width arithmetic is the usual source of silent breakage:
        # R2/R5 widen by +1, R3/R6 narrow by -1.

        for o in R.ontologies:
            assert o.graph.check_integrity() == []
            for p in R.pointers:
                assert CORRESPONDING_POINTER(o, p) != null   # §5

        # An EMPTY ontology set is legitimate and deliberately NOT asserted:
        # it means "no valid model", which the driver surfaces instead (§7.5).


10. Notes for the implementer
------------------------------

  - THE READING LAYER NEVER REDUCES. It only builds terms, bottom-up. Nothing in
    §§2–4 calls `optimal_lambda.normalize` or any rule in `rules.py`. Pointer
    invalidation there is trivial: only the acted-on pointer changes.

  - THE ONTOLOGY LAYER REDUCES, and that is where every hazard lives: R1/R4 plus
    silent cascades run on ontology graphs while pointers are held across them.
    §6 is the contract that makes it safe.

  - POINTERS ARE EDGES PLUS OCCURRENCES. Reflection's pointers designate the
    aux-port edges of the fan-in — the occurrences of the shared subject — and
    carry the cast type. Implementing them as node handles, or as edges without
    an occurrence, breaks §4 and §1 respectively.

  - THE SILENCE TEST IS ONE LINE: `fan.main == fan.width("principal") - 1`
    separates syntactic λ/@ from sharing. Get it wrong and either the ontology
    runs ahead of the reading (too silent) or nothing reduces at all (too loud).

  - COST. Each reading step is local: O(size of grafted material), except a
    closed-reading operand, which is O(|cr.graph|) under the copy default. The
    ontology set is the real cost centre — enrichment branches per pointer per
    step and the closed-reading case multiplies by |cr.ontologies| (O10).

  - PERSISTENCE. `kg_store.save_readings_db` must be extended to hold ontology
    sets (O7). Abstractions already persist separately, via
    `term_utils.append_lambda_term`.
